00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.030
Leah: So, what are the disadvantages
of writing complicated code?

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.160
Teams of professional programmers
often collaborate on projects,

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.640
where it is very common that other
people will need to view their code.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.160
This could be to review it, extend
it, or even just to understand it,

00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:20.230
in order to write other parts of the
program which are dependent on it.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.390
To try to mitigate some of the
problems caused by complicated code,

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.930
there are many coding practices
and conventions which have been

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.960
developed to try to standardise code.

00:00:30.639 --> 00:00:33.230
One example is ‘PEP8 for Python’.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.890
This is a document that provides
guidelines and best practices

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:40.510
to improve the readability and
consistency of coding in Python.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.770
For more information on
this check out our weblink.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.070
Jack: So, what are the
disadvantages of complicated

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.729
coding and the effects it can have.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:52.040
First let’s look how
this affects Variables

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.670
If ‘variable names are too
long’ this can clutter code

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.860
If ‘variable names are not descriptive’,
they are ambiguous to what the

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.510
variable holds or its function.

00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:05.050
If ‘variable names are not
standardised’, you might not be

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:09.450
able to tell what sort of variable
it is, for example local or global.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:14.810
There are however conventions for this
in some languages such as capitalization,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.210
prefixed or suffixed with an underscore.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.340
Leah: So, now let’s look at
how this affects ‘Functions’

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.570
Say a ‘function name is undescriptive’
you might not be able to tell what

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.020
the function is supposed to do.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:31.620
‘Functions may become bloated,
non-specific, and have side effects’.

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:35.460
Functions should generally
serve one purpose, and not

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.110
affect other code sections.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.750
It can make debugging difficult
when a function is not constrained.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:44.270
‘Functions are not generalised’.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:48.890
If functions are not written to be
general, you may end up with many

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.860
functions which serve a similar purpose,
and this can make code confusing.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:59.749
‘Too many lines of code are replaced with
functions.’ Replacing sections of code

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:04.649
with functions can be useful when this
particular operation needs to occur a lot.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:10.710
However, this can confuse code and
complicate debugging if the function is

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:13.670
only called once or is only a single line.

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:17.460
Jack: Now let’s look at the
what the effect is on Comments

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:19.969
‘Comments are missing in code’.

00:02:20.589 --> 00:02:23.750
If there are no comments in a
confusing section of code, then

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.120
its purpose may be unclear.

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:30.920
‘Comments describing functions are
missing, unstandardised or too long’.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:34.590
Many languages have conventions
which describe a function.

00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:38.730
This usually includes a short
description of what it does, and the

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.699
data types, and also short descriptions
of the inputs, and the outputs.

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.230
This is called a 'docstring’ and
if it is missing, unstandardised,

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:50.740
or too long, it can take longer
for coders to resolve problems.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.780
As a program develops, it
often becomes more complex.

00:02:55.259 --> 00:02:59.239
It is therefore crucial, especially in
the early stages, to keep code clean

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:03.690
and accessible in order to ensure that
it is maintainable and understandable

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.059
when someone comes back to it.

